{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["מזי רעב — its interpretation according to its context is as in its Targum. And if it is Talmudic language, it is like נתמומז מוחיה דדין." "ולחומי רשף — hail and fire from the heavens, like רשפי [אש]. And the primary interpretation of the word is a term for flying, as it is written: ״ובני רשף יגביהו עוף״. That 'He shattered the flaming arrows of the bow' — like the expression ״מחץ יעוף יומם״." "וקטב מרירי — cut down and stabbed by brigands who embitter and kill people. Like עון פלילי — the sin of a judge who renders judgment upon it. And 'He places in the פלילים' — judges are called thus, because they judge others. So too מרירי — one who embitters others. And this is 'מקטב ישוד צהרים' — for they are brigands. As it is written: ״בחור שודד בצהרים״ — for so is the way of brigands, to fight at midday. And so it says: ״אשדוד בצהרים יגרשוה״."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rashbam, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {0 "The Aramaic Talmudic phrase means 'his brain was melted by it'; Rashbam draws the parallel because both involve 'מזי' in the sense of consuming/melting.", 1 "The bracketed [אש] appears to supply the word missing in the lemma citation of רשפי אש (Psalms 76:4). The last sentence connects רשף to arrows flying, reading 'flaming arrows' as flying missiles.", 2 "Rashbam reads מרירי as an active participle like פלילי, one who acts upon others — 'one who embitters,' parallel to 'one who judges.' The scriptural citations are drawn from Psalms and Proverbs to establish the pattern of brigandry at midday."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:39:56.695117861Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}